WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.540
Malcolm Grant: I would like to move now to
item six on the agenda, which is our normal

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:17.590
two-part paper, dealing first of all with
NHS England Corporate Performance, and then

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:22.490
NHS Performance. I would like to invite Karen
to introduce part one, please.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:28.589
Karen Wheeler: Thank you, Malcolm. This is
to provide the Board with an assurance around

00:00:28.589 --> 00:00:38.210
how NHS England is doing on its business plan
priorities for 2015/2016. We have been putting

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:44.390
in place a series of stock-takes throughout
the year, culminating in a stock-take at the

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:52.960
end of the year, to confirm both that those
programmes were on track to deliver the targets

00:00:52.960 --> 00:01:00.809
for 2015/2016, as our business plan commitments;
and secondly, importantly, since our business

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:07.720
plan for 2016/2017, which we have recently
published, builds on those ten priorities

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:15.590
so that we will be taking those into this
financial year and probably next, very substantially,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:21.770
that those stock-takes have assured us that
they are on track to continue to develop and

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:28.720
deliver against our priorities for the next
year. Ultimately, they will play their part

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:37.619
in the Five Year Forward View targets that
we are all very keen to deliver.

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:42.079
This just provides you with that assurance.
We have provided you with all the detail on

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:51.880
all the programmes, but I have just pulled
out two where we are not as green as we are

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.969
on the rest. I am not going to say very much
more on that. I think the two others, child

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:05.229
protection information and care dot data;
again, they are not individually priorities,

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:09.430
but they are part of a national portfolio
of programmes that we report against. So I

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:14.350
am happy to take any questions on those, but
it is largely to provide you with that assurance

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:21.260
that we are well set up for the delivery of
this in the coming year.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:29.690
David Roberts: You will be aware, Malcolm,
that the Audit Committee has been through

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:38.220
the portfolio with Karen, and I would observe
that the degree of central programme management,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.380
oversight and control has stepped up materially
in the last 12 months.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.500
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.660
Richard Barker: The Board members will have
the performance report for March, which is

00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:00.040
in our packs. March, of course, is also the
final month of the business year for the NHS,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.390
so it is a good time to reflect on the whole
year, as well as just the last month and the

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.890
last few months. In terms of the whole year,
firstly, I would just like to talk about A&amp;E

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.099
performance and 18‑week performance. Simon
referred to this in his introduction; in both

00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:21.440
cases, over the whole year, well over 90%
of patients have been delivered within the

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.950
targets concerned: that is a four-hour wait
for A&amp;E, and an 18‑week wait, obviously,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:35.700
for the elective side of our work.
If I could take A&amp;E first, though: although

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.090
we have delivered 91.9% for the year-end,
it has been quite challenging in the last

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:46.370
three months, and we have seen performance
levels below 90%, which is not something that

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.710
we are pleased with, and we are urgently trying
to address that and look at the root causes

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:55.380
of that deterioration and to put them right.
It is worth saying, though, that whilst there

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.199
have been workload increases that have contributed
to that, they are broadly within the parameters

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:06.989
that we would have planned for, and the deterioration
of performance is not overall. So 20 systems,

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:11.549
for example, have been responsible for 50%
of the deterioration on A&amp;E performance. So

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:15.860
it is important that we target those areas
that are particularly struggling and understand

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:19.410
that many parts of the system operate fairly
well.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:25.760
I think another point we need to make is that,
even though the headline performance has deteriorated,

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.360
evidence around operational pressures and
severe long waits show that they have been

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.550
very few this this winter; so in other words,
we are performing better in terms of the extremes,

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:41.020
but the average performance has drifted out.
One way of describing that is that for every

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:44.920
10% deterioration against the target, it is
about 20 minutes’ additional wait for a

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:49.000
patient in A&amp;E, and that is what we were dealing
with: we are dealing with a flow problem that

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.600
needs to be brought more closely together
with shorter waiting times, but also making

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.800
sure that we can treat people safely and avoid
any extremely long waits. I think the latter

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.690
of those has been achieved successfully throughout
the winter.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:11.500
On the 18-week waiting times: again, March,
we had about 3.5 million people waiting, and

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:16.600
only 8.5% of those had waited over 18 weeks.
Again, the vast, vast majority of patients

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.750
had been seen well within the 18-week target.
Again, it is similar to the A&amp;E position:

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:29.850
20 trusts responsible for about 1.7% of the
gap; so in other words, if those 20 trusts

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:35.320
had achieved their targets, the overall England
performance would have been 91.7%, so 1.7%

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:39.880
higher. So for both A&amp;E and RTT, we have a
mixed picture: the overall performance is

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:44.340
poor, but we have some target areas where
we really need to improve fast, as well as

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.030
seeing general improvements across the board.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:55.280
In terms of some better news: on cancer, the
62-day cancer wait. In March, we achieved

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:00.590
83.8%, which is 3% higher than the figure
we achieved in February, so we are making

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:05.350
progress on the cancer waits.
In some other areas, we have made some excellent

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:10.600
progress across the year, particularly in
the areas of mental health. So the improved

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:15.870
access to psychological therapies service,
for example, we set ourselves a target of

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:21.670
a 15% population coverage rate on that. Our
expectation is that that would be the level

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:27.060
of the population that needs those therapies,
and we achieved a 16.6% coverage rate across

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:32.280
the year, so we are getting access to those
patients who need to receive them. We set

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.720
ourselves a target of a 50% recovery rate,
and we are on track for that: we achieved

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.780
47.9% towards the end of the year, and it
continues to improve month on month.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.950
Finally, we set ourselves the target that
75% of patients would be seen within six weeks;

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:51.890
not 18 weeks, but six weeks for this service,
and we achieved 84.3%, so some good results

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:57.170
in psychological therapies services.
Finally, on dementia services, we set ourselves

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:03.280
the target that we would diagnose two thirds
of the population that is expected to have

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:08.880
dementia, and we achieved that, with a 67.5%
performance by year-end.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.570
Overall, it is a mixed picture. Some good
performance in the mental health arena; some

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:17.910
challenging performance in the acute side
of things. Overall, for the year, there has

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:22.460
been strong performance in terms of nine out
of ten patients seen, and I think you also

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:27.370
have to give credit to all our staff both
inside and outside of hospitals for achieving

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:31.900
that, set against the broad increase in in
workload: around 2% on the emergency side

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:38.150
and 3% on the waiting list side. That is the
summary of the report, Chairman.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:45.220
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Richard. The access
requirements of the NHS constitution are amongst

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:50.520
the most demanding in the world for an entire
population. Other systems have insured groups

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:58.040
for whom there are other access requirements.
What will concern everybody, I think, is the

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:03.750
drift away from observance of those; it is
almost as if is non-observance is the new

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:07.410
normal.
Just taking A&amp;E, for example, there are still

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:14.960
some trusts who are meeting the 95% requirement
of the constitution. Are there things that

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:19.800
are being transferred in terms of knowledge
and learning, from how they organise their

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.670
patient flow and their staff, which through
the UAEC Review or elsewhere are being transmitted

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:32.190
into being used in a mentoring and supportive
way for other trusts that are still struggling?

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:38.070
Richard Barker: The answer is yes, but the
whole purpose of the Urgent and Emergency

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:42.010
Care Review is that we knew that, without
changes to the way we manage emergency care,

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:46.600
the demographic pressures we were facing would
bring problems that we are experiencing. So

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.720
the best systems have adopted some of the
practices identified in the review; some have

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:55.430
a way to go to achieve that. One reason why
we have been under pressure in the last two

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:59.950
or three months is some unpredictable things
have happened: we have a late onset of flu,

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:05.680
so that has created more bed pressures. In
terms of bed capacity itself, whereas in previous

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.430
years we just would have bought more beds
to absorb more pressures, it is less possible

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:14.670
to do that now, for a couple of reasons. One
is workforce pressures, and secondly it is

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:18.320
not right to open extra beds if you do not
have the right safety in there, the right

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.350
number of nurses per patient. Those things
have been difficult.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:28.100
Finally, there have been pressures in relation
to discharge, particularly in the social care

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:33.760
end, where there has been a 34% increase in
discharges while waiting for social care.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:39.160
All of those things are risks, and the best
organisations have managed those well; they

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:44.660
have adopted new processes and reformed. Many
organisations have yet to do it. The challenge

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:49.550
for us to bring forward aspects of the Urgent
and Emergency Care Review, share the good

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:55.670
practice of those who are achieving successfully,
and embed that across the board, and that

00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:00.280
is the key to us turning performance around
and making the improvements we need to.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:04.610
Malcolm Grant: Increasingly, in close partnership
with NHSI?

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:08.100
Richard Barker: Absolutely.
Michelle Mitchell: Thank you, Richard, for

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:13.870
your clear explanation of some of the causes
and possible solutions. Could you tell us

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:18.630
whether you think some of the deteriorating
performance is a trend, or whether it will

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:22.920
get better by the end of the year?
Richard Barker: Well, we have clearly observed

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:32.200
a trend in both A&amp;E performance and RTT 18‑week
performance, and this has been happening for

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:36.960
around two years. Without wanting to repeat
the answer to the previous question, we knew

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:42.700
there was going to be more pressure, and hence
the need to make the changes we need to make,

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:46.380
and we really do need to accelerate those
changes, now.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:51.180
As I described in the overall report, we need
two sets of tactics really. One is to bring

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:56.690
rapid support to those systems that are struggling
most, and it is a relatively small number

00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:04.420
across England: there are 170 Type-1 A&amp;Es
in England, and the 20 we described causing

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:10.340
40–50% deterioration are quite a small percentage
of the 170. Working with NHS Improvement,

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:15.510
we are doing our best to offer support and
resource. We have the Emergency Care Improvement

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:21.030
Programme, and we are expanding its coverage
from 25 to 40 systems, and that would cover

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:25.300
the 20 we are talking about. So we really
need to bring resource from the best practices

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:30.640
across England, helping those systems to see
and adopt that practice. Then of course, we

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.960
have to ensure that that is actually taken
up and followed through.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:37.350
Michelle Mitchell: You have confidence that
the numbers will improve?

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:42.560
Richard Barker: As long as we do what we have
planned to, that should happen as a natural

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.100
consequence: we bring the improvement techniques,
people adopt them, and we have certainly seen

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:48.750
from the evidence of the best that that can
bring results.

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:59.279
David Roberts: I think, Simon, this is from
the majority of the citizens’ perspective,

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:03.550
A&amp;E performance, RTT performance. We have
done well on other things, let us be clear;

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:10.000
it is not a complete bad news story. However,
on the big things there is real concern. Therefore,

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.460
I wonder whether you could share with the
Board some of the thinking that you have with

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:21.960
Jim on how we change this trend line and get
to an improving position? Recognising that

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:27.000
this is not going to change overnight.
Simon Stevens: I think what Jim would say

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:36.370
is that the principal purpose of NHS Improvement
is what it says on the tin, i.e., improvement.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:44.529
So looking out to next March, 2017, their
expectation is that the NHS provider sector

00:12:44.529 --> 00:12:50.540
will be in a better position on those performance
measures than it was in March 2016, and that

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:56.880
is part of what we expect to see given that
we have funded activity growth through the

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:03.140
commissioning system and the contracting round
for this coming year, and made available the

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:10.880
£1.8&nbsp;billion on the sustainability fund,
and there is this specific set of trajectories

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:16.029
that are being agreed with individual organisations
based on what is a realistic improvement level

00:13:16.029 --> 00:13:18.609
for them individually.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:29.080
Malcolm Grant: Richard, Karen, thank you verymuch for that.

